## 6. Race Condition in Parallel State Updates

### ❌ WRONG - Reading State Outside Functional Updates in Parallel Operations

```typescript
const refreshExercises = useCallback(async (planId: string) => {
  const response = await getExercises({ trainingPlanId: planId });
  const exercises = response.data || [];
  
  // BUG: Reading state OUTSIDE the updater function
  const currentPlanData = state.planData[planId];
  updateStateAndSave({
    planData: {
      ...state.planData,
      [planId]: {
        ...currentPlanData,  // This is stale if another update runs in parallel!
        exercises,
        isLoaded: true
      }
    }
  });
}, [state.planData, updateStateAndSave]);

const refreshSavedWorkouts = useCallback(async (planId: string) => {
  const response = await getSavedWorkouts({ trainingPlanId: planId });
  const savedWorkouts = response.data || [];
  
  // BUG: Also reading state OUTSIDE the updater function
  const currentPlanData = state.planData[planId];
  updateStateAndSave({
    planData: {
      ...state.planData,
      [planId]: {
        ...currentPlanData,  // This is also stale!
        savedWorkouts,
        isLoaded: true
      }
    }
  });
}, [state.planData, updateStateAndSave]);

// When called in parallel:
await Promise.all([
  refreshExercises(planId),    // Reads state at T0: {exercises: [], savedWorkouts: []}
  refreshSavedWorkouts(planId) // Also reads state at T0: {exercises: [], savedWorkouts: []}
]);

// Result: Last write wins, one update overwrites the other!
// If refreshExercises finishes last, savedWorkouts becomes [] again!
```

**Why it fails:**
- Both functions read `state.planData[planId]` at the SAME TIME (T0)
- Both see the same old state before either update completes
- API calls complete at different times (T1, T2)
- `refreshSavedWorkouts` updates: `{exercises: [...], savedWorkouts: [1,2,3]}` ✅
- `refreshExercises` updates with OLD state from T0: `{exercises: [...], savedWorkouts: []}` ❌
- Last write wins, so workouts disappear!

### ✅ CORRECT - Use Functional Updates for All Parallel Operations

```typescript
const refreshExercises = useCallback(async (planId: string) => {
  const response = await getExercises({ trainingPlanId: planId });
  const exercises = response.data || [];
  
  // Use functional update to read CURRENT state at update time
  updateStateAndSave((prevState: State): State => {
    const currentPlanData = prevState.planData[planId];
    return {
      ...prevState,
      planData: {
        ...prevState.planData,
        [planId]: {
          ...currentPlanData,  // Now reads CURRENT state, not stale!
          exercises,
          isLoaded: true
        }
      }
    };
  });
}, [updateStateAndSave]); // No state.planData dependency needed!

const refreshSavedWorkouts = useCallback(async (planId: string) => {
  const response = await getSavedWorkouts({ trainingPlanId: planId });
  const savedWorkouts = response.data || [];
  
  // Also use functional update
  updateStateAndSave((prevState: State): State => {
    const currentPlanData = prevState.planData[planId];
    return {
      ...prevState,
      planData: {
        ...prevState.planData,
        [planId]: {
          ...currentPlanData,  // Also reads CURRENT state!
          savedWorkouts,
          isLoaded: true
        }
      }
    };
  });
}, [updateStateAndSave]); // No state.planData dependency needed!

// When called in parallel:
await Promise.all([
  refreshExercises(planId),
  refreshSavedWorkouts(planId)
]);

// Both updates properly merge their changes!
// Order doesn't matter - both see each other's updates
```

**Key principles:**
1. **Always use functional updates** when operations might run in parallel
2. **Never read shared state outside** the updater function
3. **Remove state from dependencies** to prevent unnecessary re-creation of callbacks
4. Functional updates guarantee you see the latest state at update time

**Real-World Example:**
```typescript
// Parallel data refresh after AI actions
const handleActionExecuted = useCallback(async () => {
  if (planId) {
    await Promise.all([
      refreshExercises(planId),      // Updates exercises
      refreshSavedWorkouts(planId)   // Updates savedWorkouts
    ]);
    // Both updates must use functional setState to avoid race condition!
  }
}, [planId, refreshExercises, refreshSavedWorkouts]);
```

**Symptoms of this bug:**
- UI briefly shows updated data, then reverts to old data
- Data "disappears" after successful operations
- Inconsistent state after parallel operations
- Works fine when operations run sequentially
- Page refresh shows correct data (from server)

